{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git show --format=fuller --stat f35c65c2'","aggregated_output":"git: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-oMOPPVrX' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-04 17:55:16.400 xcodebuild[27322:109274619]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-04 17:55:16.626 xcodebuild[27322:109274617] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-EMi09aP2' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-04 17:55:17.249 xcodebuild[27327:109274657]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-04 17:55:17.475 xcodebuild[27327:109274656] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ncommit f35c65c263ae9f14e5c5a41368929586b8864616\nAuthor:     Sonny To <son.c.to@gmail.com>\nAuthorDate: Wed Nov 4 17:18:12 2026 +0700\nCommit:     Sonny To <son.c.to@gmail.com>\nCommitDate: Wed Nov 4 17:18:12 2026 +0700\n\n    fix(yang.clojure): full-shape data maps invoke as data; the id-language gate\n    \n    The r3 gate still keyed the dereference on the seal's format, so a\n    three-entry data map carrying any 64-character string under :seal\n    routed to admission and died at cell/get instead of answering its map\n    lookup: genuine references are issued as plain maps (engine issue-ref),\n    and authenticity is the VM's heap+seal check, not any shape.\n    \n    The dereference candidate test now also requires the :id to be a\n    keyword of the engine's heap id language: gen-id mints every cell id\n    from one counter as :cell-N, so a full-shape map whose :id is not such\n    a keyword names no cell the task could ever have minted - it can never\n    be genuine, and its invocation is the ordinary map lookup on both\n    evaluators, literal or assoc-built at runtime. Every genuine reference\n    passes the test (its :id IS :cell-N), so admission still receives\n    exactly the genuine candidates: live entries dereference through to\n    the call, and a genuine-but-dead reference keeps the standing\n    not-callable refusal, unchanged.\n    \n    A candidate that passes with a wrong seal remains a capability\n    counterfeit and fails closed at the VM's check-ref! exactly as any\n    forged reference does anywhere in the system - the residual this round\n    cannot remove: check-ref! throws a host exception through every\n    guest-level catch (the entry's raise/escape protocol carries guest\n    conditions only), so no compiled program can observe the authenticity\n    failure to fall back on it. Erasing the residual needs a non-fatal\n    authenticity probe published by yin.vm (for example a cell/live?\n    primitive or a probe effect whose handler answers authentic-ref?); that\n    is a VM-surface ruling for the seat, outside this round's boundaries.\n    The fallback grants only what the value already is - a plain lookup -\n    never a dereference, so the D2 witness bar is unchanged.\n    \n    Regressions: the JVM corpus gains the full-shape cases (literal and\n    assoc-built, answering their stored values on both evaluators), a data\n    map's call-eligibility answering false, the genuine live/dead legs over\n    a spliced cell/new, and the residual pinned as the VM's\n    dead-or-forged refusal; the matrix gains the full-shape literal and\n    runtime-built donor legs. The r3 shapes (four entries, short seal,\n    bound literal) stay green as the trivially-data cases.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc    | 115 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++---------------\n test/yang/clojure_test.clj    | 111 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----\n test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj |  25 +++++++++\n 3 files changed, 201 insertions(+), 50 deletions(-)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"agent_message","text":"**REJECT pending an explicit owner or architect ruling on the residual.** Round 4 fixes the reported `:some-key` examples, but it does not implement the requested fallback on failed authenticity. A source map can still be routed to `cell/get` and terminate with a host-level reference error instead of returning its map value. That is a source-semantics defect, not a demonstrated capability bypass; accepting it as a limitation is an architectural decision.\n\nThe discriminator in [clojure.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1245) requires exactly three entries, `:type :cell-ref`, an `:id`, a 64-character **string** seal, and a keyword ID whose **name starts with** `cell-`. It does not validate a numeric suffix, a namespace, hexadecimal seal contents, heap membership, or authenticity. Thus it accepts more than the engine’s actual `:cell-N` ID language. For example, invoking this ordinary data map with `:id` is statically routed to admission and then `cell/get`, rather than returning `:cell-7`:\n\n```clojure\n({:type :cell-ref\n  :id :cell-7\n  :seal \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\"}\n :id)\n```\n\nThe test at [clojure_test.clj](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2131) pins this very candidate’s dead-or-forged refusal when no matching cell exists. An ID such as `:cell-customer` also passes the prefix gate despite being outside the minted numeric language.\n\nThe VM checks heap membership and the stored seal before dereferencing ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:335)). An incorrect seal cannot gain cell capability through this route. A map containing an actual live ID and its valid seal is itself a copy of the bearer reference and can exercise that existing capability; the gate does not create the seal. A failed candidate can nevertheless abort execution at host level. A non-fatal authenticity probe is the proposed way to distinguish it, but adding such a VM surface is outside this round’s authorized boundary and requires the requested ruling.\n\nThe other review findings are:\n\n- The D2 token packet still checks vector length before indexing and authenticates the compiler-held token; descriptor admission still compares the constructor witness ([clojure.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1098)). The task-lived globals and foreign-cell `kind-of` paths are present. I found no §8.10 call-node surgery, foreign `:code` extraction, or language-pair wrapper in the reviewed paths.\n- The new literal and `assoc`-built tests assert concrete values, including through the TypeScript leg ([cross_lang_test.clj](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt/test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj:170)). Genuine live references are tested through admission. The test labeled “genuine-but-dead” actually creates a **live cell containing non-callable data**; it establishes not-callable refusal, not the behavior of a swept reference. The latter’s host refusal follows from the VM check, but that test does not establish it.\n- The **round 4 commit** changes only `yang/clojure.cljc` and two `test/yang` files, with no VM or `yang/core/call.cljc` changes. The **whole branch diff** from its rebase base `811ac5ec` also includes `yang/clojure/prelude.cljc`, `yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc`, and `test/yang/call/conformance.clj`; therefore the stated three-file boundary is true for the final commit, not for the whole branch. The local `master` has advanced beyond that base, so its current merge-base and diff are not identical to the remote-current state reported in the request.\n\nThe reported remote results are useful evidence for the tested cases; I did not rerun them. The named Clojure–TypeScript skip should remain **deferred**, rather than declared fully closed, until the owner accepts the documented map-invocation limitation or rules on an authenticity probe and the resulting fix."}}
